A||N||16||Ozzy||Duke Heads??||||5||
Z||000000||Ozzy||02-19-2000||02:32 AM||||I have an '84 SE 2m4 as well as my '88 Coupe now. The top end on my Coupe is noisier than the '84. Also I'm wanting to do a port job on the Coupe.<P>Question is, are the 2 heads interchangeable with a minimum of fuss? That way I can keep the Coupe rollin' while I take the head of the '84 and work it and make a one move swap also getting a quieter head at the same time.<P>Of course, being that this makes total sense and covenience I'm sure there is some problem...still a little cynical even though my Coupe is running really well at the moment!!<P>Ozzy||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||02-19-2000||11:24 AM||||did you check the 88 for a loose rocker?||||reg||1
Z||000002||Ozzy||02-19-2000||01:16 PM||||No, not yet ogre. I know these are hydraulic but should they be adjusted if the clearance between the lifter and the rocker is excessive?<P>Are the heads interchangeable??<P>Ozzy||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phaeton||02-19-2000||01:56 PM||||Hydraulics run zero clearance, should be no click at all once oil pressure is achieved.<BR>I saw on another thread that the heads do interchange, and some casting are better than others.||||reg||1
Z||000004||David||02-19-2000||05:04 PM||||If your '88 is a distributor-less engine I think that you will find that the head bolt pattern is different from an earlier engine.  If I remember correctly, it has to do with the end bolts.  Two bolts have been moved.  This problem prevented me from using one of these later heads on my '86 when rebuilt the engine.  Had to buy a reman head and throw a good late model head away. <p>[This message has been edited by David (edited 02-19-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||batboy||02-19-2000||05:20 PM||||If your lifters are clattering, you have problems. Yes they can be adjusted which is a temporary fix, but it's better than your valve train getting beat to crap by excessive clearance. The Duke's are commonly know to be a little noisy on the upper end. I believe the heads are interchangeable, but it's not the head making the noise. Probably the valve train. The 2.5 is also known to eat up the timing gear on the cam. Replace this if you have it apart.||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||02-19-2000||08:19 PM||||2 things generally will make the valves loose.<P>1. the nut has worked loose on the rocker. This is fairly common and SLIGHT tightening should take care of it.<P>2. A lifter or rocker stud has failed. If the rocker nut needs more than 1/4 to 1/2 turn then this has to be checked out. If the lifter has colapsed and you tighten the nut  to take up the slack you will likely damage the engine.<P><BR>The cam gear has also been a common problem in these motors. It can only be fully inspected by removing the timing cover. The impression I have is that this has not been quite as big a problem in the later motors. Part of the reason is that sometime in 86 GM changed to a roller lifter cam what operates under less rotational drag than flat face lifters. Less drag means less force on the cam gear. They still wear but not quite as fast.||||reg||1
Z||000007||batboy||02-19-2000||10:47 PM||||Ogre's right about a collapsed lifter should not be adjusted (it should be replaced immediately), but if this was the case, it ought to be making a terrible clacking noise. Usually it will bend a pushrod and mushroom the valve until the rocker comes loose and stops working entirely. The rocker arm nuts are supposed to be self locking, but I've seen lots of stress cracks on them before that caused them to loosen up over time.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Cooter||02-19-2000||11:50 PM||||I have a rocker and "stud" (let it go Monkeyman!) off of a Duck and they are not adjustable. The rocker is held in by a shouldered bolt that tightens fully against the head. I was going to use a set of roller rockers that I had in the closet, but because of the lack of a standard adjustment nut/stud combo, this was not practical. BTW the Duck uses rockers that are more like big block than a small block. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Phaeton||02-20-2000||12:26 AM||||Oldsmobile made a 'net lash' V8, saves money during assembly, but what a pain.  On the V8 aftermarket adjustable stud kits are available, somebody should make them for the 2.5, or else one of our members has fabricated his own set out of existing parts.||||reg||1
Z||000010||batboy||02-20-2000||01:17 AM||||Well, I learned something new today. I knew the 2.8 engines used a similar rocker arm assembly as the Chevy small block, so I guess I assumed the 2.5 was the same too. The 88 will have the roller cam and lifters where the 84 won't. On small blocks, I believe the pushrod is a different length on the roller engines. I don't know about the 2.5, but those heads and valvetrains might not be as interchangeable as I first thought.<P>Phaeton, I've replaced a few of those old style Oldsmobile aluminum rockers in my day. Seems like it was not such a great idea.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Ozzy||02-20-2000||02:31 AM||||Well, the "ticking" noise I posted about a short time ago has returned also. I changed the oil and added a small ammount of Marvell's Mystery Oil to it. It gradually subsided but I took the car for a ride today (about 100 miles) and stopped to pick up something at the store. When I fired the engine up again the tick appeared as bad as before. Guess my run of trouble free operation is done. The engine has been running really good.<P>So I guess I will have to take the valve cover off tomorrow and have a look see! I was only wanting to see if the heads were interchageable to do a port job for another possible improvement and now I possibly have a problem instead.<P>If it turns out to be a collapsed lifter, can I change them out with the engine in the car?? (Remember...'88 Duck) Is there any way I can verify a bad lifter??<P>Ozzy||||reg||1
Z||000012||862M4inCA||02-21-2000||05:54 AM||||You should be able to check to make sure the lifter is good by doing a leakdown test, but in my experience it's better to just replace them all as a set. As for access to the lifter galley my '86 is setup to easily get to the lifter galley after removal of the intake manifold, should be similar for the 88's. Just make sure you keep EVERYTHING separate and in order. ||||reg||1
Z||000013||batboy||02-21-2000||08:25 AM||||Ozzy figured out he had a broken pushrod guide and a bent pushrod.He has started another topic thread to explain this.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 02-21-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||theogre||02-21-2000||09:17 AM||||Check the rocker nuts anyway. even the shoulder type can back off. If it's backed off allot then check the stud.<P>A colapsed lifter can just rattle like bloody hell. A lifter thats jammed at full hight usually breaks something in short order. It all depends on just how the lifter guts have failed what if any damage will be done.<P>the lifters <I>should</I> be servicable with the engine in. Looks like the best way to get to them is to take off the intake manifold. Time consuming, but easier than yanking the motor.<P>replace the lifters as a set. check the rollers very carefully. if there is any damage to a roller then the cam will have to go. If these where flat face I'd say replace the cam with the lifters. Since the rollers generate far less wear they should be ok to just replace the lifters. If you have any doubt replace the cam & bearings. There's no sense spending money on the lifters if you don't trust the cam. you'll have to drop the back of the craddle to get enough room to get the cam out I think.<P>Follow the helms book directions for handling the push rods and anything else you take off. Push rod <B>must</B> go back in the way they come out.<P>I usually don't take the rockers off and leave them off. I take them off one at a time, check them and the rest of the hardware over, and then put them back on the stud. then you can't get them mixed up. just start the nuts a few threads then you can turn the rockers to drop the rods back in.<P>As I recall the 4 cyl is a ball rocker. to make them adjustable you'd need to change the balls and nuts, maybe the rockers to. Could be fun finding parts but doable if parts can be found. I'm not sure what good it would do. Another posibility might be to simply shorten the stops on the nuts a little bit. then you have to watch out you don't bind the rocker against the head. I don't know how much room there is for adjustment. I didn't pay that much attention to it when I did the valve cover gasket.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Ozzy||02-26-2000||11:08 PM||||Well, I just got finished putting in new rockers, push rods, lifters & push rod guides. Man, what a pain these things are to work on sometimes. I had to drop the manifold off to get at the lifters.<P>I have a theory... When the Pontiac engineers were developing the Fiero, they took every wrench & socket in the Craftsman catalogue and would choose a wrench to remove a bolt and then place something in the way of the last 1/2" end of that wrench, voiding the chance of using that wrench!!!<P>Anyway, it was worth doing 'cause I'm forced to change the intake manifold gasket which was something I was never real comfortable with. I also spent a bunch of time cleaning gunk and grunge up and cleaning up wiring etc. I was not able to start the little beast because the battery drained from the radio and interior lights running so as soon as I jump it and get it going tommorrow, I will report if the head is quieter and what, if any improvements. (I'm sure you will all sleep well then huh?)<P>Ozzy<p>[This message has been edited by Ozzy (edited 02-27-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||Ozzy||02-27-2000||10:48 PM||||So I got the lil' red monster going this morning. The head is still noisy in my opinion. The tick obviousley dissapeared but the same ol' chatter is there. Compared to my '84 SE anyway. It has 110k and top is much quieter. I can see why though...with the new rockers torqued down, they are fairly loose when there is no upward pressure from the push rod. Is it possible to shim the underneath side of the bolt head? <P>Anyhow, it's running good and I have a nice clean engine with no oil leaks.<P>Next challenge...serpentine auto belt tensioner! (ugh!)<P>Ozzy||||reg||1
